WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.248
How fast is an algorithm?

00:00:02.248 --> 00:00:05.208
The most direct answer: implement it

00:00:05.208 --> 00:00:06.844
run it, and time it.

00:00:06.844 --> 00:00:10.116
In Java, read System.nanoTime() before

00:00:10.194 --> 00:00:11.674
and after the call

00:00:11.674 --> 00:00:13.700
and print the difference.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:15.622
Experiments are useful

00:00:15.622 --> 00:00:17.536
but they have two problems.

00:00:17.536 --> 00:00:19.724
First, they only tell you about

00:00:19.792 --> 00:00:21.228
the inputs you tried.

00:00:21.228 --> 00:00:24.099
A search can be fast on your test arrays

00:00:24.168 --> 00:00:26.218
just because the value happens

00:00:26.287 --> 00:00:27.722
to be near the front.

00:00:27.722 --> 00:00:30.183
Second, they are hard to make fair.

00:00:30.183 --> 00:00:32.713
To compare two algorithms by timing

00:00:32.713 --> 00:00:35.516
both must be written by the same person

00:00:35.516 --> 00:00:38.387
in the same language, with the same care

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:40.301
on the same machine and the

00:00:40.369 --> 00:00:42.147
same Java virtual machine.

00:00:42.147 --> 00:00:45.223
Change any of these, and the numbers change

00:00:45.292 --> 00:00:47.479
a time measured on a laptop says

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:49.530
little about a supercomputer.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.402
So instead we analyze the algorithm itself

00:00:53.402 --> 00:00:56.760
on paper: we count its basic operations.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.988
A basic operation is a step simple

00:01:00.066 --> 00:01:02.636
enough that a computer does it in

00:01:02.714 --> 00:01:04.505
a fixed amount of time.

00:01:04.505 --> 00:01:07.231
Arithmetic, like a + b * c.

00:01:07.231 --> 00:01:08.711
Assigning a value.

00:01:08.711 --> 00:01:11.748
Indexing an array, or reading a field.

00:01:11.748 --> 00:01:15.019
Calling a method counts as one operation

00:01:15.019 --> 00:01:17.978
the call itself, not the work inside.

00:01:17.978 --> 00:01:20.860
Returning, and comparing two values.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.335
The assumption is that each basic

00:01:23.399 --> 00:01:25.331
operation takes constant time.

00:01:25.395 --> 00:01:28.228
We don't care whether an addition takes 0.

00:01:28.292 --> 00:01:31.061
3 or 0.5 nanoseconds

00:01:31.061 --> 00:01:33.250
we only count how many there are.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.439
The count becomes the running time, T(n),

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:40.125
a function of the input size n.

00:01:40.125 --> 00:01:43.734
It takes all possible inputs into account

00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:46.084
not only the ones we tried

00:01:46.084 --> 00:01:49.190
and it is the same on every machine.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:52.065
Which input of size n do we count for?

00:01:52.065 --> 00:01:53.659
Usually the worst case

00:01:53.726 --> 00:01:55.985
the input that makes the algorithm

00:01:56.051 --> 00:01:57.181
do the most work.

00:01:57.181 --> 00:01:59.307
A guarantee is what users need

00:01:59.373 --> 00:02:01.233
if a search engine sometimes

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.894
takes 100 seconds

00:02:02.961 --> 00:02:05.087
it does not help that it usually

00:02:05.153 --> 00:02:06.548
takes a tenth of one.

00:02:06.548 --> 00:02:08.940
The average case is hard to define

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.734
average over which inputs?

00:02:10.734 --> 00:02:13.458
And the best case is rarely interesting

00:02:13.525 --> 00:02:15.385
almost any algorithm is fast

00:02:15.451 --> 00:02:16.780
on some lucky input.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.157
Our first example sums an array.

00:02:20.157 --> 00:02:23.916
Line 1 is 1 assignment: 1 operation.

00:02:23.916 --> 00:02:25.839
Line 2 is the loop.

00:02:25.839 --> 00:02:28.636
It is really for i = 0

00:02:28.636 --> 00:02:31.084
i < n; i++.

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:33.794
It assigns i = 0 once.

00:02:33.794 --> 00:02:37.465
It tests i < n, n + 1 times

00:02:37.465 --> 00:02:40.000
because the last test fails.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.622
And it increments i, n times.

00:02:42.622 --> 00:02:44.720
That is 2n + 2.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.597
Line 3 runs n times

00:02:47.597 --> 00:02:52.020
and each time it indexes A[i] and adds

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:52.757
2n.

00:02:52.757 --> 00:02:55.390
Line 4 is 1 return.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.420
In total, T(n) = 1

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:02.537
+ (2n + 2) + 2n + 1

00:03:02.537 --> 00:03:04.260
= 4n + 4.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.346
Let's run it for n = 2

00:03:07.346 --> 00:03:09.517
and count with a counter.

00:03:09.517 --> 00:03:12.440
tempSum = 0: that is 1.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:15.525
i = 0: 2.

00:03:15.525 --> 00:03:20.274
Test i < n: true, 3.

00:03:20.274 --> 00:03:26.176
Add A[0]: an index and an addition

00:03:26.176 --> 00:03:27.039
5.

00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:29.630
i++: 6.

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:31.856
Test again: 7.

00:03:31.856 --> 00:03:33.941
Add A[1]: 9.

00:03:33.941 --> 00:03:35.609
Increment: 10.

00:03:35.609 --> 00:03:39.986
The third test, 2 < 2, fails

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:40.820
11.

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.987
And the return: 12.

00:03:42.987 --> 00:03:45.831
4 * 2 + 4 = 12

00:03:45.831 --> 00:03:47.490
as the formula says.

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.824
The note's Java version, SumCount

00:03:50.905 --> 00:03:53.173
increases a counter at every

00:03:53.254 --> 00:03:54.550
basic operation

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.710
and compares it with 4n + 4.

00:03:57.710 --> 00:03:59.897
They agree for every size

00:03:59.897 --> 00:04:02.246
up to n = 1000

00:04:02.246 --> 00:04:04.190
4004.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.820
Draw 4n + 4 against n

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:09.440
a straight line.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:13.348
The counted values, for sizes 0, 1

00:04:13.348 --> 00:04:16.970
2, 5 and 10, sit exactly on it.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.283
Counting every operation

00:04:19.365 --> 00:04:21.083
like this is tedious

00:04:21.165 --> 00:04:23.046
and the exact constants

00:04:23.128 --> 00:04:24.928
depend on conventions.

00:04:25.009 --> 00:04:26.973
Other books count i++

00:04:27.054 --> 00:04:28.936
as 2 operations.

00:04:28.936 --> 00:04:30.736
The constants change

00:04:30.736 --> 00:04:32.944
but what matters does not

00:04:32.944 --> 00:04:35.480
the cost is proportional to n.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.639
For many algorithms

00:04:37.639 --> 00:04:40.245
the size is not the whole story.

00:04:40.324 --> 00:04:42.141
Linear search looks for

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:43.957
a value x in an array

00:04:43.957 --> 00:04:46.879
and returns its index, or -1.

00:04:46.879 --> 00:04:48.695
Here are 9 numbers.

00:04:48.695 --> 00:04:50.117
Search for 3

00:04:50.117 --> 00:04:53.197
the first comparison already finds it.

00:04:53.197 --> 00:04:54.460
1 comparison.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:58.412
Search for 20: the index walks along

00:04:58.412 --> 00:05:00.521
comparing each element

00:05:00.521 --> 00:05:02.894
and finds it at index 6

00:05:02.894 --> 00:05:05.090
after 7 comparisons.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.181
Search for 5, which is not there

00:05:08.181 --> 00:05:11.531
every one of the 9 elements is compared

00:05:11.531 --> 00:05:14.120
and the search returns -1.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.891
The program LinearSearchCount confirms it

00:05:17.891 --> 00:05:21.234
and adds a fourth search, for 27.

00:05:21.234 --> 00:05:24.425
3 needs 1 comparison; 27

00:05:24.425 --> 00:05:27.844
the last element, and 5, the missing one

00:05:27.844 --> 00:05:29.060
both need 9.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:30.886
So for the same n

00:05:30.886 --> 00:05:33.919
the cost ranges from 1 comparison

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:37.525
the best case, when x comes first, to 9

00:05:37.525 --> 00:05:41.050
the worst case, when x is last or missing.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:42.888
In the worst case

00:05:42.888 --> 00:05:45.780
linear search makes n comparisons

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.260
with the loop and the return

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:51.814
about 3n + 3 basic operations.

00:05:51.814 --> 00:05:53.880
Again proportional to n.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:55.260
Your turn.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:57.900
Linear search on an array of 1000

00:05:57.980 --> 00:06:00.060
distinct numbers.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:03.420
If x is equally likely to be any of them

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.540
how many comparisons does a successful

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.460
search make on average?

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:10.810
Pause the video and work it out.

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:15.879
If x is at index i

00:06:15.879 --> 00:06:19.093
the search makes i + 1 comparisons.

00:06:19.093 --> 00:06:21.993
So the average is 1 + 2 +

00:06:21.993 --> 00:06:25.521
up to 1000, divided by 1000

00:06:25.521 --> 00:06:27.951
1001 over 2

00:06:27.951 --> 00:06:29.910
500.5.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:32.650
About half of the worst case

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:35.040
and still proportional to n.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:36.935
This is what we meant

00:06:37.017 --> 00:06:39.325
the average case is often as

00:06:39.407 --> 00:06:41.220
bad as the worst case.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:43.348
Now a harder count.

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:46.607
maxSubArraySum finds the largest sum

00:06:46.688 --> 00:06:49.540
of a contiguous piece of the array

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:52.880
by trying every start i and every end j.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:54.387
For each i

00:06:54.387 --> 00:06:58.279
the inner loop runs n - i times

00:06:58.279 --> 00:07:02.486
n times, then n - 1, down to 1.

00:07:02.486 --> 00:07:06.378
Call that sum S: n * (n + 1)

00:07:06.378 --> 00:07:07.430
over 2.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:11.892
Lines 1 to 3 are counted as before.

00:07:11.993 --> 00:07:14.600
The inner loop's own tests

00:07:14.701 --> 00:07:19.114
add 2S + 2n; line 5 costs 2S

00:07:19.114 --> 00:07:20.819
and line 6, S.

00:07:20.819 --> 00:07:25.232
Line 7 runs only when the test is true

00:07:25.232 --> 00:07:27.940
so we charge it at most S.

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.310
Adding up the lines

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:33.531
we get 6S + 5n + 5

00:07:33.531 --> 00:07:36.931
and with S equal to n² + n

00:07:36.931 --> 00:07:40.420
over 2, that is 3n² +

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:42.120
8n + 5.

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:44.255
This is an upper bound

00:07:44.255 --> 00:07:46.274
not the exact worst case.

00:07:46.274 --> 00:07:48.913
It charges line 7 every time

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:50.776
but no input makes that

00:07:50.853 --> 00:07:52.483
test true every time

00:07:52.561 --> 00:07:54.734
the very first test compares

00:07:54.812 --> 00:07:57.839
A[0] with itself, and it is false.

00:07:57.839 --> 00:08:01.099
The exact worst case is a little smaller

00:08:01.099 --> 00:08:03.350
and much harder to work out.

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.456
The program MaxSubArrayCount counts

00:08:06.528 --> 00:08:08.837
the operations for a few arrays.

00:08:08.837 --> 00:08:10.136
For 6 elements

00:08:10.136 --> 00:08:12.734
143 operations

00:08:12.734 --> 00:08:15.765
below the bound of 161.

00:08:15.765 --> 00:08:18.869
For 1 to 10: 339

00:08:18.869 --> 00:08:21.250
below 385.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.129
Every count stays below the bound.

00:08:24.129 --> 00:08:25.394
This is typical

00:08:25.394 --> 00:08:27.551
exact counts are often hard

00:08:27.551 --> 00:08:30.080
while a good upper bound is easy.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:31.307
Even so

00:08:31.388 --> 00:08:34.462
3n² + 8n + 5

00:08:34.542 --> 00:08:36.807
is more detail than we want.

00:08:36.807 --> 00:08:38.748
When n is 1000

00:08:38.748 --> 00:08:42.226
the 3n² term is 3 million

00:08:42.307 --> 00:08:44.410
while 8n + 5 is

00:08:44.491 --> 00:08:46.432
8005.

00:08:46.432 --> 00:08:48.292
Next to the first bar

00:08:48.292 --> 00:08:50.556
the second one is a sliver

00:08:50.556 --> 00:08:52.740
the lower terms are noise.

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:55.654
What matters is that the count

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:58.390
grows like n²: double n

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:01.210
and the work roughly quadruples.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:03.636
Asymptotic analysis makes

00:09:03.719 --> 00:09:05.297
grows like precise.

00:09:05.297 --> 00:09:08.120
That is the rest of this lecture.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:09.542
To sum up.

00:09:09.542 --> 00:09:12.234
One: we count basic operations

00:09:12.234 --> 00:09:14.506
each taking constant time

00:09:14.506 --> 00:09:17.872
as a function T(n) of the input size.

00:09:17.872 --> 00:09:21.491
Two: the input matters, not only its size

00:09:21.491 --> 00:09:24.773
there is a best case and a worst case

00:09:24.773 --> 00:09:27.634
and we usually analyze the worst.

00:09:27.634 --> 00:09:30.747
Three: counts like 4n + 4

00:09:30.747 --> 00:09:34.366
or 3n² + 8n + 5

00:09:34.366 --> 00:09:37.058
are more detailed than we need

00:09:37.058 --> 00:09:40.340
only the fastest-growing term matters.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:43.652
Try this: in these two nested loops

00:09:43.652 --> 00:09:45.956
the inner one runs i times.

00:09:45.956 --> 00:09:47.437
For n = 10

00:09:47.437 --> 00:09:50.810
how many times does count++ run?

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:54.305
Next, we make grows like precise

00:09:54.305 --> 00:09:55.544
with Big-Oh

00:09:55.544 --> 00:09:58.880
an upper bound on the growth rate.

